    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>The City of San Marcos enforces the 2021 International Codes and the 2020 NEC, in effect since April 1, 2023.</li>
<li>A local amendment says a roof covering replacement permit is not required unless the new roof is heavier than the existing covering.</li>
<li>Nearby cities run their own permit offices, and Hays County does not issue residential building permits in unincorporated areas.</li>
<li>Code requirements do not automatically mean your insurer pays for upgrades. Ordinance or law coverage, and its limit, usually decides that.</li>
<li>Code items get paid more often when they are documented clearly: the code section, the photo, the measurement and the cost.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>When a hailstorm or windstorm damages a home in San Marcos, the repair has to meet the building codes in force today, not the codes that were in place when the house was built. That gap between "put it back the way it was" and "build it the way the code now requires" is one of the most common sources of disagreement on property claims. This guide explains how <strong>San Marcos building codes and insurance repairs</strong> fit together: which codes the city enforces, when a reroof needs a permit, what ordinance or law coverage does, and how to document code items so they are not quietly left off an estimate.</p>

<p>It is written for homeowners and business owners in San Marcos, Hays County and nearby Central Texas communities. It is general information, not legal advice, and every claim depends on the policy language and the facts of the loss.</p>

<h2>Which building codes San Marcos enforces</h2>

<p>The City of San Marcos adopted the 2021 editions of the International Codes, along with the 2020 National Electrical Code, through Ordinance 2023-09. According to the city’s <a href="https://www.sanmarcostx.gov/1289/Codes-Ordinances" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Codes &amp; Ordinances page</a>, these codes have been in effect since <strong>April 1, 2023</strong>. As of this writing, the city website still lists the 2021 editions as current.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Code (2021 edition unless noted)</th><th>What it governs</th><th>Why it can matter on a claim</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>International Residential Code (IRC)</td><td>One- and two-family homes and townhouses</td><td>Roof coverings, underlayment, flashing, framing and many items in a typical home repair</td></tr>
<tr><td>International Building Code (IBC)</td><td>Commercial, multifamily and other non-IRC buildings</td><td>Commercial roofs, structural repairs and life-safety items</td></tr>
<tr><td>International Energy Conservation Code (IECC)</td><td>Energy efficiency</td><td>Insulation, windows and some roof or wall assemblies when replaced</td></tr>
<tr><td>International Existing Building Code (IEBC)</td><td>Repairs and alterations to existing buildings</td><td>Sets how much of a damaged building must be brought up to current code</td></tr>
<tr><td>IMC, IPC, IFGC</td><td>Mechanical, plumbing and fuel gas</td><td>HVAC, water heaters, piping and venting after fire, freeze or water losses</td></tr>
<tr><td>International Fire Code (IFC)</td><td>Fire safety</td><td>Commercial and multifamily repairs, alarms and access</td></tr>
<tr><td>International Property Maintenance Code (IPMC)</td><td>Minimum standards for existing properties</td><td>Habitability issues and unsafe conditions after a loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>National Electrical Code (2020)</td><td>Electrical wiring and equipment</td><td>Rewiring after fire or water damage, panel and circuit protection</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The city also adopted a swimming pool and spa code and several local amendments. Two of those amendments come up often on claims: the reroof permit rule described below, and a requirement that all foundations be designed by a licensed engineer.</p>

<h2>The San Marcos reroof permit rule</h2>

<p>Many Texas cities require a permit for every roof replacement. San Marcos took a different approach. In its amendments to both the IRC and the IBC, the city says that roof covering replacement permits are <strong>not required unless the weight of the replacement roof is heavier than the existing roof covering</strong>.</p>

<p>In practice, that means a like-for-like composition shingle replacement on a house in a San Marcos subdivision often will not need a permit. Moving to a heavier covering, such as from shingles to concrete or clay tile, may. If you are not sure, the city publishes a Permit Requirement Determination Form that lists re-roofing as an example of work it can rule on, or you can contact the Permit Center directly.</p>

<div class="callout"><span class="callout-title">City of San Marcos Permit Center</span><p>630 E. Hopkins Street, San Marcos, TX 78666 · 512-805-2630 · permitinfo@sanmarcostx.gov. Online permit applications go through MGO Connect (mgoconnect.org). See the city’s <a href="https://www.sanmarcostx.gov/1000/Permit-Center" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Permit Center page</a> for current details.</p></div>

<h3>No permit does not mean no code</h3>

<p>This is the point that trips people up. The model codes generally say that an exemption from a permit does not authorize work that violates the code. A reroof that needs no permit is still expected to be installed the way the adopted IRC or IBC and the manufacturer’s instructions require. When a contractor bids the job properly, those requirements show up as line items. When an insurance estimate leaves them out, the gap shows up as an underpayment.</p>

<h2>Permits outside the San Marcos city limits</h2>

<p>The San Marcos rules apply inside the city. Properties nearby may be under a completely different authority:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Other cities.</strong> Kyle, Buda, Wimberley, Dripping Springs, New Braunfels, Lockhart and Seguin each have their own permit offices. They may have adopted different code editions or their own amendments, including different reroof permit rules. Check with the city that covers your address before assuming the San Marcos rule applies.</li>
<li><strong>The San Marcos ETJ.</strong> The city’s ordinance requires a building permit for projects in the extraterritorial jurisdiction that receive City water, wastewater or electric service.</li>
<li><strong>Unincorporated Hays County.</strong> Hays County does not issue residential building permits or enforce a residential building code in unincorporated areas. It does handle septic (OSSF), development and floodplain permits, and it applies the 2018 International Fire Code to commercial and multifamily construction. See <a href="https://www.hayscountytx.gov/595/Development-Permits" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hays County Development Permits</a>.</li>
</ul>

<p>Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes page</a> collects these resources in one place, and the <a href="/service-areas/">service areas</a> pages note the permit office for each community we serve.</p>

<h2>Ordinance or law coverage: who pays for code upgrades</h2>

<p>Here is the key insurance point: <strong>a code requirement does not automatically mean the insurer pays for it.</strong> A standard property policy pays to repair or replace covered damage. Many policies exclude, or limit, the extra cost of complying with an ordinance or law. That extra cost is usually covered by <strong>ordinance or law coverage</strong>, which pays, up to its limit, for increased costs to comply with building codes when repairing covered damage.</p>

<p>Ordinance or law coverage is often a separate, limited coverage or endorsement. On many residential policies it is expressed as a percentage of the dwelling limit. Some commercial policies split it into parts, such as the value of undamaged portions that must be torn down, the cost to demolish them, and the increased cost of construction. Some policies do not include it at all.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Where payment often comes from</th><th>What to check</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Item is a normal part of a like-kind repair (for example, standard underlayment under new shingles)</td><td>Often the main dwelling coverage</td><td>Whether the estimate includes it at all</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code requires something the old roof or system never had</td><td>Usually ordinance or law coverage, if the policy has it</td><td>The endorsement, its limit, and whether repairs must be completed first</td></tr>
<tr><td>Undamaged parts must be removed or upgraded to meet code</td><td>Ordinance or law coverage, depending on wording</td><td>Whether the policy covers undamaged portions and demolition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code item unrelated to the covered damage</td><td>Often not covered</td><td>Whether the requirement is triggered by the repair of covered damage</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Many policies pay ordinance or law benefits only after the work is actually done, and some apply them on a replacement cost basis only when repairs are completed. Read the endorsement, and see our guide to <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a> for how holdbacks work.</p>

