<h2>Common code-driven line items on insurance repairs</h2>

<p>Code items vary by building type, roof covering, the code edition your city adopted, and the manufacturer’s installation instructions. Below are items that frequently come up on San Marcos-area claims. Whether any of them apply to your property is a question for your contractor and the local building official.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/roof-inspection-damage-details.webp" alt="Inspector on a shingle roof examining damage, with close-up insets of granule loss and flashing" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Flashing, edge metal and underlayment are easy to miss in an estimate because they are hidden once the roof is finished.</figcaption></figure>

<h3>Roofing</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Drip edge.</strong> The IRC requires drip edge at the eaves and rake edges of asphalt shingle roofs. Some older roofs were built without it, so it may be missing from both the old roof and the insurer’s estimate.</li>
<li><strong>Underlayment.</strong> The adopted IRC requires underlayment under roof coverings, installed to code and manufacturer specifications. Watch for estimates that price only a thin felt where the system or manufacturer calls for something different.</li>
<li><strong>Tear-off versus overlay.</strong> The IRC generally does not allow a new roof covering to be installed over two or more existing layers. If the house already has two layers, a full tear-off may be required.</li>
<li><strong>Flashing.</strong> Step flashing, counterflashing and valley flashing must be installed to code and manufacturer requirements. Reusing old, damaged flashing may not meet them.</li>
<li><strong>Roof ventilation.</strong> Replacing vents or changing the roof can bring attic ventilation requirements into play.</li>
<li><strong>Decking.</strong> Damaged, deteriorated or improperly spaced decking may need replacement before a new covering can go on.</li>
</ul>

<p>You may notice that ice barrier is not on this list. The IRC ties ice barrier requirements to areas with a history of ice forming at the eaves, which is not typical in San Marcos. If an estimate or bid includes it, ask why.</p>

<h3>Other trades</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Electrical.</strong> Rewiring after a fire or water loss can trigger current NEC requirements, such as circuit protection.</li>
<li><strong>Insulation and windows.</strong> Replaced assemblies may need to meet the IECC.</li>
<li><strong>Mechanical and plumbing.</strong> Replacing a water heater or HVAC equipment after a freeze or fire can bring updated venting, clearance or connection requirements.</li>
<li><strong>Foundations.</strong> In San Marcos, all foundations must be designed by a licensed engineer under the local amendment. That can matter on major fire or flood rebuilds.</li>
<li><strong>Commercial properties.</strong> Fire code, accessibility and structural requirements can be significant. Our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page covers those losses.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to document code items on your claim</h2>

<p>Insurers tend to pay for code items when they can see exactly what is required, why it applies, and what it costs. A vague note that says "code upgrades needed" rarely gets far. Here is a practical approach:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Get the policy and endorsements.</strong> Find out whether you have ordinance or law coverage, the limit, and any conditions such as completing repairs first.</li>
<li><strong>Identify the authority.</strong> Confirm which city or county has jurisdiction and which code edition it enforces. For San Marcos, cite the 2021 codes and the city’s Codes &amp; Ordinances page.</li>
<li><strong>Name the requirement.</strong> Note the code section or the manufacturer instruction behind each item. Your contractor or the building official can help you identify it.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph existing conditions.</strong> Show what is there now: missing drip edge, two existing layers, deteriorated decking, undersized vents. Photos taken during tear-off are especially useful.</li>
<li><strong>Measure and price it.</strong> Each item should appear as its own line with a quantity and unit price, not buried in a lump sum.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the paperwork.</strong> If a permit is required, keep the permit, inspection results and any written correction notices from the building official.</li>
<li><strong>Submit in writing.</strong> Send code items to the adjuster as a written supplement request with the supporting documents attached.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> can help you keep the evidence organized. If code items were left out after the work started, a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">claim supplement</a> is the usual way to add them.</p>

<h2>When a San Marcos public adjuster can help</h2>

<p>Code items are a frequent reason estimates come in low, partly because they require reading the policy, the code and the estimate side by side. A <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> can review your policy for ordinance or law coverage, compare the insurer’s estimate with what the adopted codes and manufacturer instructions call for, and prepare documentation for the missing items. Public adjusters represent the policyholder only and are licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance.</p>

<p>We cannot promise any particular result, and if your dispute turns on a legal question, such as how a policy exclusion should be interpreted, a Texas attorney is the right resource. For an <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid claim</a> where code items seem to be missing, or for a <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage claim</a> you are just starting, our <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> is a no-obligation way to get a second look. You can also read our guide on <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">how to review an underpaid roof estimate</a>.</p>

<p>As a San Marcos public adjuster serving San Marcos, Hays County, and surrounding Central Texas communities, we know a common pattern on property claims: the damage is covered, but the estimate prices a repair that could not be built that way under current code. Getting the code items documented early is one of the simplest ways to close that gap. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
